-- | One piece of an interpolated string — doc 03 §11.1, plan 23.
-- |
-- | `"total: #{amount:.2}"` is two parts: the text `total: `, and an
-- | interpolation of `amount` with the spec `.2`.
export data StrInterpPart
  -- | Literal text, **ready to paste into a Rust string**.
  -- |
  -- | The lexer decodes escapes, so this has been re-escaped by the parser —
  -- | one implementation that also serves the `"""` block and the byte and
  -- | C-string literals. The emitter adds only the brace doubling, which is
  -- | not an escaping concern but a format-string one.
  = Text String
  -- | `#{expr}` or `#{expr:spec}`.
  | Interp {
    -- | An ordinary expression. The lexer lexes the interpolation's body with
    -- | the ordinary scanners, and plan 23 runs the ordinary expression parser
    -- | over those tokens — so `#{a + b}`, `#{user.name}` and `#{f x}` work
    -- | without a second grammar. Emerald accepted only an identifier here for
    -- | eleven plans and paid to widen it; this plan's §8 records that.
    expr : crate::Expr,
    -- | Rust format-spec text, **carried verbatim and never interpreted**.
    -- |
    -- | Doc 03 §11.1: *"text after `:` up to the closing brace is spec text,
    -- | passed through unchanged. A malformed spec is reported by rustc against
    -- | the interpolation's own location through the source map."* That is
    -- | AGENTS.md's rule applied to a grammar Rust already owns, and it is what
    -- | makes "every Rust format spec reachable" true with no list to fall
    -- | behind.
    spec : Option<String>,
  }
  deriving (Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)
